WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.430
Hi there, Steve Kaufmann here, and today
I wanna talk about reading, how to use

00:00:05.430 --> 00:00:07.590
reading to increase your vocabulary.

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:12.390
Um, you know, I, I always stress
the importance of varying our

00:00:12.395 --> 00:00:14.160
activities in language learning.

00:00:14.430 --> 00:00:18.510
I talked a bit about Atomic Habits
and developing good, sort of, learning

00:00:18.510 --> 00:00:22.740
habits that keep you going, uh,
without necessarily always worrying

00:00:22.740 --> 00:00:26.760
about the end of the road, you
know, fluency, whatever that might.

00:00:27.795 --> 00:00:29.175
So we have to have good habits.

00:00:29.175 --> 00:00:34.125
We have to vary the kind of activity
we do and different activities help

00:00:34.125 --> 00:00:35.985
us in different ways, obviously.

00:00:36.345 --> 00:00:40.335
So if you're in a conversation
group, you're practicing using

00:00:40.365 --> 00:00:42.315
the words that you already know.

00:00:42.405 --> 00:00:47.410
Probably you're practicing using
a small subset of the words that

00:00:47.410 --> 00:00:51.550
you understand, because our active
vocabulary is always gonna be

00:00:51.550 --> 00:00:53.920
smaller than our passive vocabulary.

00:00:54.130 --> 00:00:57.220
But at least if you have good
listening comprehension, you understand

00:00:57.220 --> 00:00:58.360
what other people are saying.

00:00:58.990 --> 00:01:03.730
When we are listening here, again, we can
only understand the words that we know.

00:01:03.879 --> 00:01:08.560
So listening is a way of reinforcing
your passive vocabulary, improving

00:01:08.560 --> 00:01:12.375
your comprehension, all of which
is very good, good things to do.

00:01:12.555 --> 00:01:15.775
In fact, I spend more time listening
than any other activity, 'cause

00:01:15.775 --> 00:01:17.205
it's the easiest thing to do.

00:01:17.205 --> 00:01:24.075
I can do it anytime, anywhere, but reading
is where we can acquire new vocabulary.

00:01:24.615 --> 00:01:31.451
And I'm gonna talk a bit about extensive
reading versus intensive reading and

00:01:31.455 --> 00:01:33.059
how it helps us acquire new words.

00:01:33.119 --> 00:01:38.880
So in a way, the ultimate
experience in extensive reading

00:01:38.910 --> 00:01:41.220
is to read a book, a paper book.

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:42.839
You have no distractions.

00:01:43.020 --> 00:01:44.070
You can't go anywhere.

00:01:44.070 --> 00:01:46.530
You're there with the
book, words you don't know.

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:51.250
You just let them go by you and you're
experiencing reading the language,

00:01:51.250 --> 00:01:55.470
your're experiencing, converting,
you know, various characters on

00:01:55.470 --> 00:01:57.479
a piece of paper into meaning.

00:01:57.539 --> 00:01:58.690
Very valuable.

00:01:58.830 --> 00:02:02.640
I've always, you know, when I
reach the stage where I can read a

00:02:02.640 --> 00:02:07.410
novel in a language I'm learning,
I have reached a major milestone.

00:02:07.800 --> 00:02:13.110
And I very often hear people say, well,
you know, uh, I can read well, but

00:02:13.170 --> 00:02:16.680
uh, you know, I can't, uh, understand
when I hear people speaking or I

00:02:16.680 --> 00:02:17.910
have trouble using the language.

00:02:18.360 --> 00:02:19.890
And then I say to those people, do...

00:02:19.890 --> 00:02:22.440
have you read a novel in the
language you're learning?

00:02:22.620 --> 00:02:24.270
And very often the answer is no.

00:02:24.330 --> 00:02:28.500
I mean, you have to get to the point
where you can read novels and read widely.

00:02:28.500 --> 00:02:33.269
Not just newspaper articles, but
you know, a book, uh, doesn't

00:02:33.269 --> 00:02:34.200
have to be a novel, by the way.

00:02:34.200 --> 00:02:36.120
It can be a non-fiction for that matter.

00:02:36.269 --> 00:02:38.489
So that's extensive reading on paper.

00:02:38.850 --> 00:02:42.750
However, again, we have to get to
that stage because if there's too

00:02:42.755 --> 00:02:48.030
many words that you don't know, then
reading in a, you know, away from the

00:02:48.030 --> 00:02:49.920
computer is not a pleasant experience.

00:02:50.070 --> 00:02:55.170
So that's where reading online with an
online dictionary, and in particular

00:02:55.174 --> 00:02:59.670
the way we do things at LingQ, can
help you acquire the vocabulary,

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:04.440
pass the vocabulary so that you
can eventually achieve the ultimate

00:03:04.440 --> 00:03:07.500
experience in reading, which is to
grab a book and just read that book.

00:03:07.740 --> 00:03:14.430
So I consider reading online, uh,
looking words up, saving words.

00:03:15.670 --> 00:03:20.760
That sort of call it LingQ reading
experience is like mining the

00:03:20.760 --> 00:03:26.100
language, mining for gold, mining
for minerals, mining for vocabulary,

00:03:26.220 --> 00:03:27.990
bringing that vocabulary in.

00:03:28.230 --> 00:03:30.180
Initially it's in there somewhere.

00:03:30.180 --> 00:03:30.870
You can't retrieve it.

00:03:31.655 --> 00:03:36.055
You see that word again in a different
context or many different contexts.

00:03:36.075 --> 00:03:40.755
Slowly, it becomes part of your passive
vocabulary, and eventually over time,

00:03:40.905 --> 00:03:45.765
some of that vocabulary will become
active vocabulary that you can use.

00:03:46.185 --> 00:03:49.545
So extensive reading, which is by
the way, something that Stephen

00:03:49.545 --> 00:03:51.735
Krashen constantly promotes.

00:03:52.095 --> 00:03:56.489
There's all kinds of experience
which shows, even if an, if

00:03:56.489 --> 00:04:00.299
we're not consciously looking up
words, if we are reading widely,

00:04:00.299 --> 00:04:04.500
extensively, we're getting more and
more familiarity with the language.

00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:10.140
Eventually some of that vocabulary is
naturally going to become known to us as

00:04:10.144 --> 00:04:13.920
passive vocabulary be simply because we've
seen it in so many different contexts

00:04:13.920 --> 00:04:16.440
that we get a, a, a sense of the meaning.

00:04:16.769 --> 00:04:21.615
I prefer to look things up because
I can have a, an erroneous idea

00:04:21.615 --> 00:04:24.495
of what the word means and stay
with that for a long, long time.

00:04:24.675 --> 00:04:28.815
If I look it up, I may forget it,
but eventually I'll probably get to

00:04:28.815 --> 00:04:33.165
a, a correct understanding of the
scope of meaning of that word sooner

00:04:33.315 --> 00:04:38.985
if I have at least some opportunity
to see, you know, uh, a translation

00:04:38.985 --> 00:04:42.445
of that word into English or into
another language that I know very well.

00:04:43.535 --> 00:04:48.900
But even in terms of reading content,
for example, my, uh, Persian tutor

00:04:48.900 --> 00:04:53.490
sent me an e-book and I can import
that e-book, you know, essentially

00:04:53.490 --> 00:04:55.690
with one click, uh, into LingQ.

00:04:55.710 --> 00:04:57.910
So I've got the whole
e-book socked into LingQ.

00:04:57.930 --> 00:05:01.320
Now, unfortunately, I don't have
enough audio for that e-book.

00:05:01.320 --> 00:05:05.310
That would be even better because
then I could be reinforcing my

00:05:05.310 --> 00:05:10.020
mining activity with, you know,
listening while doing other chores.

00:05:10.080 --> 00:05:10.740
I don't have that.

00:05:10.740 --> 00:05:15.030
However, This book that I
got, this e-book, it's still

00:05:15.090 --> 00:05:16.770
sort of difficult for me.

00:05:17.190 --> 00:05:23.580
So, uh, I can go at it again within my
extensive reading of this book, I can

00:05:23.585 --> 00:05:25.950
go at it intensively or extensively.

00:05:26.340 --> 00:05:32.160
If I go at it sort of extensively, then
I'm on sort of page mode and I just read

00:05:32.160 --> 00:05:34.320
through looking up words and so forth.

00:05:34.590 --> 00:05:38.520
But I'll show you here how I
can go through this material

00:05:38.880 --> 00:05:43.605
intensively in sentence mode
and doing the sentence review.

00:05:43.995 --> 00:05:46.425
Now, this is more intensive activity.

00:05:46.425 --> 00:05:49.185
It's working with, uh, nuts and bolts.

00:05:49.185 --> 00:05:53.745
It's reconstituting the sentence, it's
coming, you know, focusing in on the

00:05:53.745 --> 00:05:58.605
vocabulary a bit more, putting the
sentence back together again, which

00:05:58.605 --> 00:06:02.375
forces me to think of the structure of the
language, the word order in the language.

00:06:03.195 --> 00:06:07.395
And however, this is gonna
slow down my mining activity.

00:06:07.395 --> 00:06:09.495
I'm coming across fewer and fewer...

00:06:09.900 --> 00:06:14.040
fewer, not fewer and fewer, but fewer new
words because I'm working intensively, so

00:06:15.090 --> 00:06:20.520
I always like to combine some intensive
work with some extensive work where I'm

00:06:20.525 --> 00:06:24.930
just reading, not worrying about the
words, not trying to focus in on them,

00:06:25.200 --> 00:06:26.969
not trying to focus in on the structure.

00:06:27.150 --> 00:06:29.760
I'm just reading, and yet
at other times I'm working

00:06:30.030 --> 00:06:32.130
intensively, as I will demonstrate.

00:06:32.520 --> 00:06:35.820
We have a lot of material in our library.

